Ladybird now measures a column flex item’s block size at its min-content
or max-content width when the container is sized under that constraint.
Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and Playwright’s WebKit
26.5, Chrome and Firefox make the container in the test 202px wide;
WebKit makes it 2px, as it does an inline-block holding the same item.

For a single-line column container, three functions take the largest
of the items’ own inline contributions, without measuring their
block sizes: Blink’s FlexLayoutAlgorithm::ComputeMinMaxSizes(),
WebKit’s FlexFormattingContext::minimumMaximumContentSize(),
and Gecko’s nsFlexContainerFrame::ComputeIntrinsicISize(). But
for a multi-line one, Blink’s BuildSpaceForLayout() and WebKit’s
FlexItemsAtMaxContentWidthScope measure each item at its max-content
width, which this matches.

The engines differ on the item’s height, which Chrome makes 200px and
Firefox 100px, so the test reports only widths.
